## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of memory hierarchy and cache effects, which are crucial for writing high-performance, low-latency code in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- **False sharing**: When two threads modify variables that share the same cache line, even if they access different variables, the cache coherence protocol forces invalidation and reloads
- **Cache lines**: Typically 64 bytes on modern CPUs; data is transferred between main memory and CPU caches in these blocks
- **MESI protocol**: Cache coherence protocol (Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid) that ensures consistency but introduces overhead when false sharing occurs
- **Memory alignment and padding**: Restructuring data to occupy separate cache lines eliminates false sharing at the cost of increased memory usage
- **pthreads and barriers**: Lightweight threading with synchronization primitives to coordinate threads and measure timing accurately

## Code Example
```c
// Variant A: no padding (false sharing occurs)
typedef struct { long v0; long v1; } counters_t;

// Variant B: 64-byte padding per counter (no false sharing)
typedef struct { 
    long v0; 
    char pad0[64 - sizeof(long)]; 
    long v1; 
    char pad1[64 - sizeof(long)]; 
} counters_padded_t;
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical concurrency on bare-metal MCUs: interrupt-safe data sharing, wrap-around handling, and overflow behavior in a tight, deterministic path.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffers with power-of-two sizing
- Volatile indices and memory visibility between ISR and main code
- Minimal interrupt masking to avoid latency spikes
- Edge-case testing for wrap and overflow

## Code Example
```c
#define N 32
#define MASK (N-1)
static uint8_t buf[N];
static volatile uint8_t head = 0; // consumer index
static volatile uint8_t tail = 0; // producer index

int enqueue_byte(uint8_t b) {
  uint8_t next = (tail + 1) & MASK;
  if (next == head) return -1; // full
  buf[tail] = b;
  // memory barrier if available
  tail = next;
  return 0;
}

int dequeue_byte(uint8_t *out) {
  if (head == tail) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[head];
  head = (head + 1) & MASK;
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-producer/multi-consumer safely?
- What tests would you run on a simulated interrupt workload to prove correctness?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of fixed-size memory allocators, O(1) operations, and basic safety guarantees in constrained memory environments—core knowledge for systems programming and embedded development.

## Key Concepts

- **Fixed-size block allocator**: Divides a pre-allocated pool into uniform blocks, eliminating external fragmentation
- **In-block free list**: Stores next-pointer directly within each free block, using only 8 bytes per block
- **O(1) operations**: Both allocate and free simply manipulate a head pointer—no traversal required
- **8-byte alignment**: Block size is naturally aligned; allocator returns properly aligned addresses
- **Double-free protection**: Tracks block state via flags or list membership checks
- **Fragmentation testing**: Measure allocation success rates under various patterns; track internal fragmentation (fixed by design, none) and external fragmentation (none for fixed-size)
- **Memory safety testing**: Validate bounds, check for leaks via allocation/free counts, fuzz with random operations

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define POOL_SIZE (256 * 1024)    // 256 KiB
#define BLOCK_SIZE 128            // 8-byte aligned
#define NUM_BLOCKS (POOL_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

typedef struct {
    uint32_t next;                // Index of next free block
} free_block_t;

static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE];
static uint32_t free_head = 0;    // Head of free list
static bool allocated[NUM_BLOCKS]; // Track block state

void init_pool(void) {
    free_head = 0;
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < NUM_BLOCKS; i++) {
        free_block_t* block = (free_block_t*)(pool + i * BLOCK_SIZE);
        block->next = (i + 1 < NUM_BLOCKS) ? i + 1 : 0xFFFFFFFF;
        allocated[i] = false;
    }
}

void* allocate(void) {
    if (free_head == 0xFFFFFFFF) return NULL;

    uint32_t index = free_head;
    free_block_t* block = (free_block_t*)(pool + index * BLOCK_SIZE);
    free_head = block->next;
    allocated[index] = true;

    return (void*)block;
}

bool free(void* ptr) {
    if (ptr == NULL || ptr < pool || ptr >= pool + POOL_SIZE) return false;

    uint32_t index = ((uint8_t*)ptr - pool) / BLOCK_SIZE;
    if (index >= NUM_BLOCKS || !allocated[index]) return false; // Double-free protection

    allocated[index] = false;
    free_block_t* block = (free_block_t*)(pool + index * BLOCK_SIZE);
    block->next = free_head;
    free_head = index;

    return true;
}
```

## Testing Strategy

**Fragmentation**: With fixed-size blocks, external fragmentation is impossible. Test by repeatedly allocating and freeing in random patterns; all allocations should succeed until the pool is exhausted.

**Memory Safety**:
- Verify bounds on allocate/free calls
- Test double-free detection returns false
- Test free of invalid pointers returns false
- Run with address sanitizer to catch overflows
- Track allocation count vs. free count to detect leaks

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crash-consistency with persistent memory, memory ordering, and minimal synchronization in a high-throughput queue. Emphasizes per-slot sequencing, proper flush order, and recovery logic after power loss.

## Key Concepts
- Persistent memory semantics and crash-consistency
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Flush/fence strategies (pmem_persist, sfence, clflushopt)
- Recovery protocol after crash or reset

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  uint8_t payload[16];
  uint64_t seq;
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
  slot_t *slots;
  uint64_t head; // producer position (mod N)
  uint64_t tail; // consumer position (mod N)
  uint64_t mask; // N-1
} pmem_queue_t;

int enq(pmem_queue_t *q, const void *data);
int deq(pmem_queue_t *q, void *out);
```

