## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates expertise in persistent memory programming, crash-consistency guarantees, and low-level synchronization primitives. It assesses whether a candidate can design systems that survive power failures while maintaining correctness.

## Key Concepts
- **Persistent memory semantics**: Understanding durable writes, memory persistence guarantees, and the difference between DRAM and PMEM
- **ABA prevention**: Per-slot sequence counters enable detection of reused slot positions across crash scenarios
- **Flush/fence ordering**: CLWB/PCOMMIT for durability, SFENCE for ordering, ensuring writes are persisted in the correct sequence
- **Crash recovery**: Epoch-based reclamation and state reconstruction after power loss
- **Microbenchmark design**: Measuring both crash-recovery latency and in-memory operation throughput

## Code Example
```cpp
struct alignas(64) Slot {
  std::atomic<uint64_t> seq;
  uint8_t payload[120];
};

struct PMEMQueue {
  Slot* slots;
  std::atomic<uint64_t> head;
  std::atomic<uint64_t> tail;
  size_t cap;

  bool enqueue(const void* data, size_t len);
  bool dequeue(void* data, size_t* len);
  void recover();
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of low level synchronization, memory ordering, and reusability. A barrier is fundamental in frame sync, parallel tasks, and NUMA awareness, but many candidates overfit to simple mutex patterns.

## Key Concepts

- C11 atomics and memory ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier design
- Reuse safety and race avoidance
- Spin-wait costs and pause hints
- Microbenchmark methodology

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <sched.h>
volatile atomic_int arrivals;
volatile atomic_int generation;

void barrier(int N){
  int gen = atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire);
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&arrivals, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == N-1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&arrivals, 0, memory_order_release);
    atomic_store_explicit(&generation, 1 - gen, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire) == gen){
      sched_yield();
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for false sharing avoidance across cores?
- How would you measure livelock risk and scale beyond 16 threads?

